1
00:00:00,960 --> 00:00:03,040
 - Editing is where the magic happens.

2
00:00:03,040 --> 00:00:05,880
 It's where all that hard work, production and shoot days

3
00:00:05,880 --> 00:00:08,760
 gets laid out and put together to tell a story.

4
00:00:08,760 --> 00:00:12,360
 In many ways, editing is invisible storytelling

5
00:00:12,360 --> 00:00:14,640
 and understanding the different types of cuts

6
00:00:14,640 --> 00:00:16,320
 is key to mastering it.

7
00:00:16,320 --> 00:00:18,440
 So in this video, we're gonna dive into

8
00:00:18,440 --> 00:00:21,980
 10 important editing cuts every filmmaker should know.

9
00:00:21,980 --> 00:00:23,720
 So I don't think we can start this list

10
00:00:23,720 --> 00:00:26,520
 without the most obvious cut of them all at number one

11
00:00:26,520 --> 00:00:29,560
 and it is called, well, the cut.

12
00:00:29,560 --> 00:00:31,600
 The cut is an instant switch

13
00:00:31,600 --> 00:00:34,320
 between one shot to another, including audio.

14
00:00:34,320 --> 00:00:35,820
 You would have seen thousands of these

15
00:00:35,820 --> 00:00:38,660
 as it's the most popular cut style of them all.

16
00:00:38,660 --> 00:00:41,180
 Here you see we cut cleanly from this bar scene

17
00:00:41,180 --> 00:00:42,720
 to this office scene and we're ready

18
00:00:42,720 --> 00:00:44,360
 to take on new information.

19
00:00:45,240 --> 00:00:46,740
 Number two, the jump cut.

20
00:00:47,800 --> 00:00:50,360
 This cut is when a segment of a shot has been removed

21
00:00:50,360 --> 00:00:53,120
 and the separate ends have been spliced back together,

22
00:00:53,120 --> 00:00:56,000
 making the shot feel like it has jumped in time.

23
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:59,120
 You may also see a lot of jump cuts in talking head videos,

24
00:00:59,120 --> 00:01:02,160
 on YouTube to help remove pauses and mistakes.

25
00:01:02,160 --> 00:01:05,580
 In this Tarantino movie, the effect these jump cuts are having

26
00:01:05,580 --> 00:01:07,220
 is showing the passing of time

27
00:01:07,220 --> 00:01:09,880
 and that the characters have actually been speaking longer

28
00:01:09,880 --> 00:01:11,220
 than the screen time being shown.

29
00:01:11,220 --> 00:01:12,220
 Oh yeah.

30
00:01:12,220 --> 00:01:15,880
 Let me tell you, I just got my part for sheer luck.

31
00:01:15,880 --> 00:01:17,880
 Number three is the match cut.

32
00:01:17,880 --> 00:01:22,760
 The match cut is a cut that matches action, shape, colour, framing

33
00:01:22,760 --> 00:01:27,220
 or even audio of two shots, seamlessly bringing the viewer into the next scene.

34
00:01:31,220 --> 00:01:35,560
 The three types of match cuts are graphic with visual elements matching,

35
00:01:36,360 --> 00:01:40,520
 movement with camera, character or object movement matching,

36
00:01:40,520 --> 00:01:43,720
 and audio with the sound matches between the two scenes.

37
00:01:43,720 --> 00:01:46,680
 Mama said they'd take me anywhere.

38
00:01:46,680 --> 00:01:50,180
 She said they was my magic shoe.

39
00:01:50,180 --> 00:01:54,820
 Cuts number four and five is the J cuts and the L cut.

40
00:01:54,820 --> 00:02:00,760
 A J cut is when the audio from the next scene starts before the video cuts.

41
00:02:00,760 --> 00:02:03,720
 So the viewer hears what's coming before they see it.

42
00:02:03,720 --> 00:02:06,600
 Here is a popular J cut from the Wolf of Wall Street

43
00:02:06,600 --> 00:02:09,560
 where we begin to hear Matthew McConaughey beating on his chest,

44
00:02:09,560 --> 00:02:12,360
 leading us into the lunch scene before we visually see it.

45
00:02:12,360 --> 00:02:13,720
 Shut it, shut it, shut it.

46
00:02:13,720 --> 00:02:20,760
 And now I realise a lot of my examples all contain Leo DiCaprio.

47
00:02:20,760 --> 00:02:22,360
 That was not intended at all.

48
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:24,920
 An L cut is the opposite.

49
00:02:24,920 --> 00:02:29,720
 The audio from the current scene continues even after the visual cuts to the next.

50
00:02:29,720 --> 00:02:32,520
 You're still hearing a line of dialogue or ambient sound

51
00:02:32,520 --> 00:02:34,280
 as you're already seeing the new shot.

52
00:02:34,920 --> 00:02:38,360
 This L cut from Ant-Man continues the character's dialogue

53
00:02:38,360 --> 00:02:41,560
 while we're visually seeing a reenactment scene, bridging the two together.

54
00:02:41,560 --> 00:02:43,880
 You know, I don't mind res, man. You know, but there was a rosé that saved the day.

55
00:02:43,880 --> 00:02:44,520
 It was delightful.

56
00:02:45,080 --> 00:02:47,640
 And then he tells me about this girl, Emily, that we used to kick it with.

57
00:02:47,640 --> 00:02:49,720
 It was actually the first pair of boobs that I ever touched.

58
00:02:49,720 --> 00:02:51,800
 It's the wrong details. It's the wrong.

59
00:02:51,800 --> 00:02:53,160
 It has nothing to do with the story.

60
00:02:53,160 --> 00:02:56,440
 These cuts are incredibly useful because they create a smoother,

61
00:02:56,440 --> 00:03:00,600
 more natural flow between shots, especially in conversations.

62
00:03:00,600 --> 00:03:04,520
 They let editors guide the audience's attention, build emotional impact,

63
00:03:04,520 --> 00:03:07,400
 and instead of just feeling like a hard stop,

64
00:03:07,400 --> 00:03:10,360
 the J and L cut make the film feel more immersive.

65
00:03:11,560 --> 00:03:15,160
 All right, number six, the fade.

66
00:03:16,840 --> 00:03:22,440
 The fade is a classic, and it's when a shot dissolves or fades to or from a solid color.

67
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:25,400
 And that color is most commonly black,

68
00:03:25,400 --> 00:03:30,760
 whereas a fade to white might be used to show the character dying or in a dream.

69
00:03:31,400 --> 00:03:33,640
 Fades are commonly at the start or the end of the film,

70
00:03:33,640 --> 00:03:36,680
 and they symbolize the beginning or the end of a story.

71
00:03:37,720 --> 00:03:41,000
 Now, similar to the fade is the next transition we have.

72
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:42,520
 And this is the dissolve.

73
00:03:43,240 --> 00:03:47,880
 This, instead of fading from a color, we just fade to a new shot.

74
00:03:47,880 --> 00:03:50,840
 The dissolve is commonly used to show a passing of time,

75
00:03:50,840 --> 00:03:53,880
 either within a scene or from one scene to the next.

76
00:03:57,160 --> 00:04:00,360
 Number eight is cutting on action.

77
00:04:01,240 --> 00:04:03,320
 Cutting on action means just that.

78
00:04:03,320 --> 00:04:06,040
 You cut during the character or object's movement.

79
00:04:06,680 --> 00:04:09,080
 Basically, when the out point of shot A

80
00:04:09,640 --> 00:04:13,400
 matches the in point of shot B, it creates a seamless transition.

81
00:04:14,040 --> 00:04:17,400
 Cutting on action is another very common technique used by editors,

82
00:04:17,400 --> 00:04:33,080
 and it helps make the cuts feel smoother and more natural to the viewer.

83
00:04:35,080 --> 00:04:37,000
 Number nine is cross cutting.

84
00:04:37,960 --> 00:04:42,680
 Cross cutting is when the editor is cutting back and forth between multiple scenes,

85
00:04:42,680 --> 00:04:43,960
 usually at the same time.

86
00:04:44,920 --> 00:04:48,200
 This is a common technique used in thriller style movies.

87
00:04:48,200 --> 00:04:51,240
 It can be used when maybe two characters are on the phone to each other,

88
00:04:51,240 --> 00:04:53,080
 and you're cutting to and from each character.

89
00:04:54,120 --> 00:04:56,040
 What cross cutting allows you to do

90
00:04:56,040 --> 00:04:59,640
 is to easily tell your two stories that are happening at the same time.

91
00:05:01,240 --> 00:05:03,320
 And luckily last, we have the smash cut.

92
00:05:03,880 --> 00:05:06,040
 And this is exactly as it sounds.

93
00:05:06,040 --> 00:05:09,160
 It's a harsh, abrupt cut from one scene to the next,

94
00:05:09,160 --> 00:05:11,400
 with contrasting visuals and audio.

95
00:05:11,400 --> 00:05:14,600
 An example of a smash cut could be a loud, chaotic scene

96
00:05:14,600 --> 00:05:16,760
 that suddenly cuts to a quiet, simple one.

97
00:05:17,480 --> 00:05:19,480
 Little man, I give the watch to you.

98
00:05:26,360 --> 00:05:26,760
 There we go.

99
00:05:26,760 --> 00:05:30,920
 That's 10 common editing cuts you can use to help with your storytelling.

100
00:05:30,920 --> 00:05:34,680
 To really maximize the impact of your edits and cuts,

101
00:05:34,680 --> 00:05:38,600
 take the time to plan and storyboard them before you go out shooting.

